description:
I would like to test a file created and downloaded by clicking a DT button (csv, excel, something more custom...). Here is an example, if this is the app.R file in the folder "shiny_test":

```r
library(shiny)
library(DT)

myModal <- function() {
  div(id = "test",
      modalDialog(downloadButton("download1","Download iris as csv"),
                  br(),
                  br(),
                  downloadButton("download2","Download iris as csv2"),
                  easyClose = TRUE, title = "Download Table")
  )
}

ui <- basicPage(
  DTOutput("dtable")
)

server <- function(input, output, session){
  output$dtable <- renderDT(
    datatable(iris,
              extensions = 'Buttons',
              options = list(
                dom = 'Bfrtip',
                buttons = list(
                  "copy",
                  list(
                    extend = "collection",
                    text = 'download entire dataset',
                    action = DT::JS("function ( e, dt, node, config ) {
                                    Shiny.setInputValue('test', true, {priority: 'event'});
}")
                  )
                )
              )
    )
  )

  observeEvent(input$test, {
    print("hello")
    showModal(myModal())
  })


  output$download1 <- downloadHandler(
    filename = function() {
      paste("data-", Sys.Date(), ".csv", sep="")
    },
    content = function(file) {
      write.csv(iris, file)
    }
  )

  output$download2 <- downloadHandler(
    filename = function() {
      paste("data-", Sys.Date(), ".csv", sep="")
    },
    content = function(file) {
      write.csv2(iris, file)
    }
  )
}

shinyApp(ui, server)

```

Then I'd like to test the files directly from download1 and download2. Running the app they both return csv's. But when I use shinytest2, I can only seem to get html files. I've tried something like:

```r
library(shinytest2)
library(testthat)

test_driver <- R6::R6Class(
  "test_driver",
  inherit = shinytest2::AppDriver,
  cloneable = FALSE,
  public = list(
    width = 1159,
    height = 642,
    trigger_dt_button = function(input_id, class) {
      self$run_js(sprintf(
        "$('#%s').button('%s').trigger();",
        input_id, 
        class
      ))
    }
  )
)


test_that("test_0123", {

  app <- test_driver$new(
    name = "test_dt_download",
    app_dir = "simple_shiny/"
  ) 

file_path <- app$get_download("download1", "test.csv")
  # that results in a html file.

  app$trigger_dt_button(input_id = "DataTables_Table_0_wrapper",
                        class = "dt-button buttons-collection")
  file_path <- app$get_download("download1", "test.csv")
  # still results in a html file.

})

```

There are 2 rabbit holes I've been going down. 

1. Trying to interact with the DT directly with JS:

@jakubsob gave a great answer here for triggering selectizeInput that made me think using custom JS would be the trick:
https://github.com/rstudio/shinytest2/issues/232
And described it even better here:
https://jakubsobolewski.com/blog/shinytest2-complex-widgets/
However I've not savvy enough with JS to figure out what I should be doing (my best guess is above in the `test_driver`).

2. I guess it could be related to this issue:
https://github.com/rstudio/shinytest2/issues/357
but I could not figure out how to debug the R6 method

Any advice would be greatly appreciated. In the meantime, I'm testing output of `exportTestValues` on the underlying data, but there are some styling pieces that happen from the data to the exported file that would be nice to test.

Hello, 

As far as I know, putting `validate(need(......))` inside a reactive conductor is a valid practice. However that causes a problem with **shinytest2**: the test fails because of an error whenever one calls `app$get_value()` or `app$get$values()`.

```r
library(shiny)
library(ggplot2)
ui <- fluidPage(
  numericInput("n", "Number of rows", 10, 1, nrow(cars)),
  plotOutput("plot")
)
server <- function(input, output) {
  dt <- reactive({
    head(cars, input$n)
  })
  
  plot_obj <- reactive({
    validate(need(input$n > 0, message = "n must be strictly positive"))
    ggplot(dt(), aes(speed, dist)) + geom_point()
  })
  
  output$plot <- renderPlot({
    plot_obj()
  })
  
  exportTestValues(
    dt = dt(),
    plot_obj = plot_obj()
  )
}

shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)
```

```r
app <- AppDriver$new()
app$set_inputs(n = 0)
app$get_values()
```

